{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="content" %}
# Content design

## The audio’s structure is aligned with the intended learning outcomes

Define the intended learning outcomes before scripting or selecting the audio. Use them to determine its scope, sequence and emphasis, and ensure that explanations, examples, stories, interviews and other segments contribute to the knowledge, perspective or performance learners are expected to develop.[^constructive-alignment]

Organise spoken content into meaningful conceptual sections and clearly signal important ideas, relationships and transitions. Because spoken information is transient, avoid long uninterrupted explanations when learners need to integrate several ideas; segmentation can particularly benefit learning from spoken text.[^signalling][^transient-information]

Remove material that has no clear explanatory, evidential, contextual or motivational purpose. Do not assume that an entertaining or engaging audio format is instructionally appropriate unless its structure supports the intended learning outcomes.

[^constructive-alignment]: [Enhancing teaching through constructive alignment](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00138871 "Biggs, J. (1996). Enhancing Teaching through Constructive Alignment. Higher Education, 32, 347–364.")

[^signalling]: [Signaling as a cognitive guide in multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-0663.93.2.377 "Mautone, P. D., & Mayer, R. E. (2001). Signaling as a Cognitive Guide in Multimedia Learning. Journal of Educational Psychology, 93(2), 377–389.")

[^transient-information]: [The transient information effect](https://doi.org/10.1002/acp.2885 "Singh, A.-M., Marcus, N., & Ayres, P. (2012). The Transient Information Effect: Investigating the Impact of Segmentation on Spoken and Written Text. Applied Cognitive Psychology, 26(6), 848–853.")

I’d keep this item compact and make **recognition and selection** the main purpose of the title.

## The audio has a clear and relevant title

Use a concise, descriptive title that communicates the audio’s main topic or purpose and helps learners decide whether it is relevant to the current learning activity. The title should remain meaningful when encountered outside the immediate page context, such as in a course outline, search result, playlist or listening history.[^descriptive-titles]

Prefer specific titles that distinguish the recording from related content. Put the most important identifying information early, and avoid vague, promotional or generic titles such as **Introduction**, **Episode 3** or **Listen now** unless additional wording makes the subject clear.[^descriptive-headings]

Keep the title consistent wherever the same recording appears, while using the surrounding introductory text to explain its particular relevance when it is reused in different learning contexts.

[^descriptive-titles]: [Providing descriptive titles](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G88.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Technique G88: Providing Descriptive Titles for Web Pages. Descriptive titles should identify the subject, remain meaningful when read out of context and be concise.")

[^descriptive-headings]: [Providing descriptive headings](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G130.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Technique G130: Providing Descriptive Headings. Descriptive headings help users locate and understand content, with important distinguishing information preferably placed early in the heading.")

## A brief introductory text accompanies the audio

Briefly explain what the audio is about, why it is relevant to the intended learners and how it contributes to the current learning activity. Where useful, identify the format or speakers, the central question or topic, and any prior knowledge learners need before listening.[^pretraining]

When the same recording is reused across different courses or activities, tailor the introduction to place it in the current learning context and emphasise its specific value for that use. Keep the text concise and avoid reproducing the audio’s opening, transcript or key conclusions.

[^pretraining]: [The pre-training principle](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139547369.017 "Mayer, R. E., & Pilegard, C. (2014). Principles for Managing Essential Processing in Multimedia Learning: Segmenting, Pre-training, and Modality Principles. In The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 2nd ed. Cambridge University Press.")

## Intro and closing jingles are brief and not distracting

If a recurring sonic identity is useful, keep introductory and closing jingles very short and clearly separated from the instructional speech. A brief, recognisable sound can signal the beginning or end of the audio without delaying access to the learning content. Netflix’s short **“Ta-dum”** sonic logo is a familiar example of this approach: distinctive enough to establish identity while requiring very little listener time.[^signalling][^coherence]

Avoid extended music, repeated branding, spoken promotional material or decorative sound that has no instructional function. Do not play the outro over important concluding information. There is no universal evidence-based duration threshold: use only as much audio as is needed for recognition or orientation, and omit the jingle entirely when it provides no useful function.[^coherence]

[^signalling]: [Principles for reducing extraneous processing in multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Fiorella, L., & Mayer, R. E. (2022). Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing in Multimedia Learning: Coherence, Signaling, Redundancy, Spatial Contiguity, and Temporal Contiguity Principles. In The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press.")

[^coherence]: [The coherence principle](https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/multimedia-learning/coherence-principle/4E80B70CB76E2166B76E5653EBDE7D3E "Mayer, R. E. (2009). Coherence Principle. In Multimedia Learning, 2nd ed. Cambridge University Press. Learners generally perform better when extraneous words, sounds and music are excluded from instructional materials.")


## Audio is clear, intelligible and technically consistent

Ensure that speech and all meaning-bearing sounds are easy to understand, without clipping, excessive reverberation, intrusive background noise, distortion or abrupt changes in level. Keep loudness consistent within each recording and across related audio so learners do not need to repeatedly adjust their device volume.[^audio-quality][^loudness]

Keep music and other background sounds low enough that they do not mask speech. Where spoken learning content includes background audio, prefer removing it, allowing it to be turned off or maintaining a substantial level difference between foreground speech and background sound.[^background-audio]

Check the final encoded file—not only the production master—on representative headphones, laptop or mobile speakers and under realistic listening conditions. Follow the loudness, peak and encoding requirements of the intended distribution platform rather than applying a single universal technical target to every learning audio format.[^loudness]

[^audio-quality]: [How audio quality influences perceptions](https://doi.org/10.1177/1075547018759345 "Newman, E. J., & Schwarz, N. (2018). Good Sound, Good Research: How Audio Quality Influences Perceptions of the Research and Researcher. Science Communication, 40(2), 246–257.")

[^background-audio]: [Understanding low or no background audio](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/low-or-no-background-audio.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.7: Low or No Background Audio. For prerecorded speech, WCAG recommends no background sound, independently controllable background sound, or background sound at least 20 dB below the foreground speech, subject to defined exceptions.")

[^loudness]: [EBU R 128: Loudness normalisation and permitted maximum level of audio signals](https://tech.ebu.ch/publications/r128 "European Broadcasting Union. EBU R 128, Version 5.0. Recommendation for measuring and normalising programme loudness using programme loudness, loudness range and maximum true-peak descriptors. Its −23 LUFS target applies to its broadcast context rather than serving as a universal target for all digital audio.")

## A complete text transcript is available

Provide a complete text transcript for prerecorded audio-only content. It should contain all spoken information in the correct order, identify speakers where multiple voices are present, and describe meaningful non-speech sounds when they contribute to understanding the content.[^audio-only][^transcript-technique]

Keep the transcript easy to find from the audio player and synchronised with the current version of the recording. Use readable headings and paragraphs for longer recordings, and preserve terminology, names, numbers and other learning-relevant details accurately.

A transcript should provide equivalent access to the audio content, not merely a summary or list of key points. It can also support searching, reviewing and quoting specific passages, but learners should remain free to listen, read or combine both modes according to their needs.

[^audio-only]: [Understanding audio-only and video-only prerecorded content](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/audio-only-and-video-only-prerecorded.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 1.2.1: Audio-only and Video-only (Prerecorded). Prerecorded audio-only content requires an alternative for time-based media that presents equivalent information.")

[^transcript-technique]: [Providing an alternative for prerecorded audio-only content](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G158.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Technique G158: Providing an Alternative for Time-Based Media for Audio-Only Content. The alternative should include the dialogue and other important auditory information and identify speakers when multiple voices are present.")


## Audio length is appropriate to its learning purpose

Keep the recording focused on a coherent learning purpose and remove avoidable repetition, lengthy preliminaries and digressions. Shorter recordings can be easier to start, repeat and reuse, and listener-retention data show that unnecessary opening material is particularly costly.[^podcast-retention]

Educational audio may range from a few minutes for a focused explanation or instruction to substantially longer interviews, discussions and narratives where the additional context contributes to the learning purpose. Research has found different learner preferences across contexts, including approximately 20–30 minutes for educational podcasts in one higher-education study.[^podcast-preferences]

When a longer recording contains several distinct topics or stages, divide it into meaningful, labelled chapters rather than shortening it at the expense of necessary explanations, examples or argumentation.

[^podcast-retention]: [Using learning analytics to evaluate the clinical education podcast format](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40670-024-02011-1 "Horta, L., Ho, D., & Lau, K. H. V. (2024). Using Learning Analytics to Evaluate the Clinical Education Podcast Format. Medical Science Educator, 34. Analysis of 29,747 plays found a steep initial decline in audience retention but no specific attention limit within episodes ranging from 8 to 17 minutes.")

[^podcast-preferences]: [Soundwaves of knowledge: using podcasts to facilitate learning in higher education](https://doi.org/10.1080/09523987.2025.2533120 "Study of Swedish university students published in 2025. Participants generally preferred educational podcasts of approximately 20–30 minutes, while the authors note that appropriate podcast length depends on learning context and content.")

## The speaking format supports the learning purpose

Choose narration, dialogue, interview, discussion or another speaking format according to what learners need to understand. Use a single narrator for focused explanations, instructions and other content that benefits from a clear, linear presentation.

Use dialogue when the exchange itself contributes to learning—for example, by exposing questions, misconceptions, alternative perspectives, reasoning, clarification or feedback. Research comparing tutorial dialogue with lecture-style monologue found stronger learning from dialogue when learners could observe meaningful tutor–student exchanges and their knowledge-building processes.[^dialogue-learning]

Keep the language natural and conversational regardless of the number of speakers. Do not add artificial questions, additional speakers or scripted exchanges merely to make the recording sound more dynamic; every contribution should have a clear explanatory, evidential, contextual or motivational purpose.[^personalisation][^coherence]

[^dialogue-learning]: [Why students learn more from dialogue- than monologue-videos](https://doi.org/10.1080/10508406.2016.1204546 "Chi, M. T. H., Kang, S., & Yaghmourian, D. L. (2017). Why Students Learn More From Dialogue- Than Monologue-Videos: Analyses of Peer Interactions. Journal of the Learning Sciences, 26(1), 10–50. Studies found learning advantages for tutorial dialogue over lecture-style monologue when dialogue exposed constructive questioning and explanation.")

[^personalisation]: [Principles based on social cues in multimedia learning](https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/cambridge-handbook-of-multimedia-learning/principles-based-on-social-cues-in-multimedia-learning/093C1CDE75CF486DACD4CABC325ABE47 "Fiorella, L., & Mayer, R. E. (2021). Principles Based on Social Cues in Multimedia Learning: Personalization, Voice, Image, and Embodiment Principles. In The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press. Research supports conversational rather than unnecessarily formal instructional language.")

[^coherence]: [Principles for reducing extraneous processing in multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108894333.019 "Fiorella, L., & Mayer, R. E. (2021). Principles for Reducing Extraneous Processing in Multimedia Learning: Coherence, Signaling, Redundancy, Spatial Contiguity, and Temporal Contiguity Principles. In The Cambridge Handbook of Multimedia Learning, 3rd ed. Cambridge University Press.")


## Key insights and takeaways accompany the audio

Provide a concise summary of the audio’s central ideas, conclusions, implications or recommended actions. Keep it available alongside the recording so learners can assess its relevance before listening and quickly revisit the most important points afterwards.[^educational-podcasts]

Include only information that is actually supported by the recording and aligned with its intended learning outcomes. Do not reproduce the transcript, introduce new claims or make the summary detailed enough to replace listening when the explanation, discussion, examples or narrative are important to the activity.

Where appropriate, invite learners to recall or formulate the main points before showing the provided takeaways. Retrieval practice generally produces stronger long-term retention than simply restudying information.[^retrieval-practice]

[^educational-podcasts]: [Podcasts as pedagogy in higher education](https://doi.org/10.1111/bjet.70021 "Thomas, M. A. M. (2026). Podcasts as Pedagogy in Higher Education: A Scoping Review to Map and Advance the Field. British Journal of Educational Technology. Review of 91 publications examining the pedagogical uses of educational podcasts in higher education.")

[^retrieval-practice]: [Rethinking the use of tests: a meta-analysis of practice testing](https://doi.org/10.3102/0034654316689306 "Adesope, O. O., Trevisan, D. A., & Sundararajan, N. (2017). Rethinking the Use of Tests: A Meta-analysis of Practice Testing. Review of Educational Research, 87(3), 659–701. The meta-analysis found that retrieving previously studied information generally improves learning compared with non-retrieval study conditions.")

## Referenced data and other materials are available

Provide direct access to reports, datasets, studies, slides, templates and other resources that the audio asks learners to inspect or relies on for important claims. Place them close to the audio player and, where useful, link them from the corresponding transcript passage or chapter so learners can move between the spoken explanation and its source.

Identify each resource with a descriptive title, author or organisation, date and relevant version. For datasets and research materials, prefer persistent identifiers and link to the specific version or subset used in the audio so the evidence can be located and verified.[^data-citation]

Include materials that help learners understand, verify or apply the content rather than collecting every resource mentioned in passing. Clearly identify paywalled, restricted or unavailable sources and provide an accessible alternative where appropriate and legally possible. Use descriptive link text rather than bare URLs or generic labels such as “click here”.[^link-purpose]

[^data-citation]: [Joint Declaration of Data Citation Principles](https://doi.org/10.25490/a97f-egyk "Data Citation Synthesis Group. (2014). Joint Declaration of Data Citation Principles. FORCE11. The principles recommend citation of data supporting claims, persistent identification, access, provenance, version specificity and verifiability.")

[^link-purpose]: [Understanding link purpose in context](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/link-purpose-in-context.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.4: Link Purpose (In Context). Links should provide enough descriptive information for users to understand their destination or purpose.")

## AI-generated audio is reviewed before publication

Review every AI-generated recording against the approved source text before it is published. Verify that no words, sentences or sections are omitted, repeated, altered or invented, and that names, numbers, abbreviations, specialist terminology and multilingual passages are spoken correctly.

Listen to the final generated audio in full and check intelligibility, pronunciation, pacing, pauses, emphasis, speaker changes and overall naturalness. Correct pronunciations or regenerate affected passages where necessary; text-to-speech systems can require explicit pronunciation guidance for words they interpret incorrectly.[^tts-pronunciation]

For learning materials, prioritise ease and accuracy of understanding over how human-like the synthetic voice sounds. Human review remains important whenever pronunciation, prosody or generation errors could change meaning or provide learners with an incorrect spoken model.[^intelligibility]

[^tts-pronunciation]: [Pronunciation overview](https://www.w3.org/WAI/pronunciation/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Pronunciation Overview. W3C documents challenges and approaches for helping text-to-speech systems produce correct pronunciation, particularly where incorrect pronunciation can make content difficult to understand.")

[^intelligibility]: [Teaching for intelligibility](https://doi.org/10.1002/tesq.70120 "Crowther, D. (2026). Teaching for Intelligibility. TESOL Quarterly, 60(2), 545–559. The review distinguishes intelligibility—the accuracy with which speech is understood—from comprehensibility and perceived accent, supporting intelligibility as the primary pedagogical concern for spoken language.")

## Localized versions are available for target languages and regions

Provide localized audio when language or regional context could limit learners’ understanding or relevance. Localization may include a translated recording, a newly recorded or synthesised voice version, a localized transcript and adapted supporting materials.[^multilingual-education]

Adapt terminology, examples, measurements, dates, names, cultural references and other context where necessary rather than translating the spoken words literally. Preserve the original learning outcomes, meaning and level of detail, and avoid cultural adaptations that introduce stereotypes or change substantive claims.

Review each localized recording with a proficient speaker before publication. Check pronunciation, terminology, pacing, naturalness and correspondence with the approved source, and keep audio, transcripts and supporting materials synchronised when the original changes. Clearly identify each available language and locale so learners can select the appropriate version.[^language-tags]

[^multilingual-education]: [Languages matter: global guidance on multilingual education](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/languages-matter-global-guidance-multilingual-education "UNESCO. (2025). Languages Matter: Global Guidance on Multilingual Education. Guidance for providing inclusive and effective education in languages learners understand.")

[^language-tags]: [Understanding language tags](https://www.w3.org/International/articles/bcp47/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding BCP 47 Language Tags. Guidance on identifying language, script, region and other locale-specific language variants.")

[1]: https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/languages-matter-global-guidance-multilingual-education?hub=84624&utm_source=chatgpt.com "Languages matter: global guidance on multilingual education | UNESCO"

{% /pattern-list %}

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="interactions" %}
# Interactions design

## Audio duration is shown before playback

Display the recording’s total running time before the learner starts it, so they can assess the expected time commitment and decide when to listen. Keep the duration visible near the title or player rather than revealing it only after playback begins.

If the activity includes pauses, reflection, note-taking, discussion or another task beyond listening, show the estimated total activity time separately so the audio duration is not mistaken for the complete learning workload.

I’d change **“manageable”** to **“easy to adjust”**: it describes an observable interface capability and stays consistent with the equivalent video recommendation. 

## Longer audio includes linked timestamps or chapters

Divide longer or multi-topic recordings into meaningful sections with descriptive labels and links to their starting points. This helps learners understand the structure, locate relevant material and return directly to a particular explanation, example or discussion without searching through the entire recording.[^segmenting]

Align chapter boundaries with conceptual transitions rather than arbitrary time intervals. Use descriptive labels that communicate what each section contains, not only timestamps such as `12:30`.

Make timestamps and chapter controls operable by mouse, touch and keyboard, and keep them synchronised when the recording is edited or replaced. Where a transcript is available, link chapters or timestamps to the corresponding transcript sections where practical.

[^segmenting]: [The transient information effect](https://doi.org/10.1002/acp.2885 "Singh, A.-M., Marcus, N., & Ayres, P. (2012). The Transient Information Effect: Investigating the Impact of Segmentation on Spoken and Written Text. Applied Cognitive Psychology, 26(6), 848–853. Segmenting transient spoken information can reduce processing demands and support learning.")

## Playback speed is easy to adjust

Allow learners to slow down or speed up the audio using clearly labelled, keyboard-accessible controls. Provide the normal 1× rate and a practical range of slower and faster options while preserving the natural pitch and intelligibility of speech.[^media-controls]

Do not force accelerated playback. Faster speeds can reduce listening time but may impair learning, particularly with dense or unfamiliar material; research suggests that these costs are generally smaller at moderate increases such as 1.5× or below.[^playback-speed]

Allow slower playback where learners may need additional processing time, particularly for unfamiliar terminology, accents or content presented in a non-native language. Keep the learner’s selected speed when appropriate across related recordings.[^speech-rate]

[^media-controls]: [Media accessibility user requirements](https://www.w3.org/TR/media-accessibility-reqs/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Media Accessibility User Requirements. Guidance includes user control over the playback rate of time-based media.")

[^playback-speed]: [Increasing video lecture playback speed can impair test performance](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10648-025-10003-9 "Tharumalingam, T., Roberts, B. R. T., Fawcett, J. M., & Risko, E. F. (2025). Increasing Video Lecture Playback Speed Can Impair Test Performance: A Meta-analysis. Educational Psychology Review, 37, Article 35.")

[^speech-rate]: [Accent and speech-rate effects in English as a lingua franca](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.system.2014.07.015 "Matsuura, H., Chiba, R., Mahoney, S., & Rilling, S. (2014). Accent and Speech Rate Effects in English as a Lingua Franca. System, 46, 143–150.")

## Seeking in audio is available by mouse, touch and keyboard

Allow learners to move forwards and backwards through the audio using mouse, touch and keyboard controls. Seeking should not depend only on dragging the timeline; provide accessible buttons or shortcuts for common jumps, together with a clearly communicated current playback position.[^media-player][^keyboard]

Ensure that timeline controls and buttons have sufficiently large touch targets, visible keyboard focus and programmatically exposed labels, values and states. Learners should be able to pause, resume and seek without requiring precise gestures or pointer input.[^target-size]

For longer recordings, complement timeline seeking with chapters or linked timestamps so learners can navigate by meaning rather than by time alone.

[^media-player]: [Accessible media players](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/player/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Media Players. Web Accessibility Initiative. Guidance on accessible playback controls, including keyboard operation and seeking.")

[^keyboard]: [Understanding keyboard accessibility](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/keyboard.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 2.1.1: Keyboard. All functionality must be operable through a keyboard interface.")

[^target-size]: [Understanding target size](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 2.5.8: Target Size (Minimum). Interactive controls should provide sufficiently large targets or adequate spacing for pointer and touch interaction.")

## The transcript can be searched and used for navigation

Allow learners to search the transcript for words, phrases, speakers or other meaningful text and move directly from a search result to the corresponding position in the audio.

Synchronise the transcript with playback so selecting a passage seeks to that point in the recording and, where useful, the currently spoken passage is visually indicated as the audio plays. Keep this synchronisation accurate when the recording or transcript is revised.[^interactive-transcript]

Ensure transcript search and navigation are operable by keyboard and assistive technologies, do not rely on colour alone to indicate the current passage, and preserve ordinary text selection and copying.

[^interactive-transcript]: [Interactive transcripts](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/transcripts/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Transcripts. W3C describes interactive transcripts that highlight text as media plays and allow users to select transcript text to navigate to the corresponding point in the audio or video.")


## Listening progress is clearly visualised

Show the learner’s current playback position and total duration throughout listening, using an accessible progress indicator that updates as the audio plays. Make elapsed and remaining time easy to understand, and allow the progress control to support seeking where appropriate.[^media-player]

Clearly distinguish playback progress from completion of the learning activity. Reaching the end of the recording indicates listening behaviour, not attention, understanding or mastery.

Make progress information available to assistive technologies and do not rely on colour alone to communicate the current or completed state.[^progressbar][^use-of-colour]

[^media-player]: [Accessible media players](https://www.w3.org/WAI/media/av/player/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Media Players. Web Accessibility Initiative. Accessible media players should expose playback controls and information so they can be operated and understood with different input methods and assistive technologies.")

[^progressbar]: [WAI-ARIA progressbar role](https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria-1.2/#progressbar "World Wide Web Consortium. Accessible Rich Internet Applications (WAI-ARIA) 1.2: Progressbar Role. Defines programmatic communication of the current value, minimum and maximum values of a progress indicator.")

[^use-of-colour]: [Understanding use of colour](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/use-of-color.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 1.4.1: Use of Color. Colour must not be the only visual means of conveying information, status or state.")


## Audio can resume from where the learner stopped

Save the learner’s latest playback position and offer to continue from it when they return, while preserving the option to restart the recording from the beginning.[^task-resumption]

For longer audio, consider resuming slightly before the saved position or at the beginning of the current chapter so the learner can recover the surrounding context. Keep saved progress synchronised across signed-in devices where appropriate, and reset or migrate it when the recording is substantially revised.

[^task-resumption]: [Interruption, recall and resumption](https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-025-05000-w "Ghibellini, R., & Meier, B. (2025). Interruption, Recall and Resumption: A Meta-analysis of the Zeigarnik and Ovsiankina Effects. Humanities and Social Sciences Communications, 12, Article 962.")

## All audio interactions are tested on mobile and touch screens

Test playback, pause, seeking, volume, playback speed, chapters, transcript access and other audio controls on supported mobile devices and touch screens. Verify that controls remain visible, responsive and usable across relevant screen sizes and orientations.[^mobile-accessibility]

Do not require precise dragging, multi-touch or path-based gestures where a simpler control can provide the same function. Ensure touch targets are sufficiently large and spaced, and test that touch interaction does not interfere with scrolling or other surrounding learning activities.[^pointer-gestures][^target-size]

[^mobile-accessibility]: [Mobile accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/mobile-accessibility-mapping/ "World Wide Web Consortium. Mobile Accessibility: How WCAG and Other W3C/WAI Guidelines Apply to Mobile.")

[^pointer-gestures]: [Understanding pointer gestures](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/pointer-gestures.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 2.5.1: Pointer Gestures. Functionality using multipoint or path-based gestures should also be operable with a single pointer unless the gesture is essential.")

[^target-size]: [Understanding target size minimum](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html "World Wide Web Consortium. Understanding Success Criterion 2.5.8: Target Size (Minimum). Pointer targets should provide sufficient size or spacing for reliable operation, particularly on touch devices.")

## Playback preferences are saved and applied across audio

Remember learner-selected playback preferences, such as playback speed, volume, mute state, transcript visibility and selected language or audio version, and apply them consistently when the learner opens another recording.

Allow learners to change or reset these preferences at any time. Do not persist settings that could create an unexpected or inaccessible experience on another device or context; for example, avoid carrying over a very low volume or muted state when this would make new audio appear not to work.

Keep persistent preferences separate from listening progress. The saved playback position should follow the individual recording, while reusable preferences should apply across related audio where this is useful and technically appropriate.[^user-preferences]

[^user-preferences]: [Understanding consistent help and user preferences](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/consistent-help.html "World Wide Web Consortium. WCAG guidance emphasises predictable, consistent interaction patterns across pages and contexts; persistent media preferences should likewise reduce repeated setup rather than introduce unexpected behaviour.")

## The paused state clearly supports continued listening

When playback is paused, preserve the current position and show a clear, accessible **Continue listening** action. Keep the elapsed and remaining time visible, and where useful identify the current chapter or section so the learner can quickly recover context.[^task-resumption]

Do not use autoplay, countdowns, guilt-based messages or misleading progress cues to push the learner to continue. The paused state should make resumption easy while preserving full learner control over when to continue, seek elsewhere or stop.[^progress-feedback]

[^task-resumption]: [Interruption, recall and resumption](https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-025-05000-w "Ghibellini, R., & Meier, B. (2025). Interruption, Recall and Resumption: A Meta-analysis of the Zeigarnik and Ovsiankina Effects. Humanities and Social Sciences Communications, 12, Article 962.")

[^progress-feedback]: [The impact of progress indicators on task completion](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intcom.2010.03.001 "Conrad, F. G., Couper, M. P., Tourangeau, R., & Peytchev, A. (2010). The Impact of Progress Indicators on Task Completion. Interacting with Computers, 22(5), 417–427.")

## Completed audios are clearly distinguished

Mark recordings the learner has finished with a persistent status, such as a **Listened** label, checkmark or progress indicator. Keep the audio title and other identifying information recognisable, and do not rely on colour alone to communicate the state.

Expose the listened state programmatically so it can be identified by assistive technologies. Treat it as a record of playback completion—not evidence that the learner paid attention, understood or mastered the content—and allow the learner to replay the recording or mark it as unlistened where appropriate.


## The next learning activity is clearly emphasised after audio completion

After the audio ends, present a prominent next action, such as listening to the next recording, reviewing key takeaways or supporting materials, or completing a retrieval, reflection or application activity.[^active-learning]

Keep the next action relevant to the learning sequence and easy to reach by mouse, touch, keyboard and assistive technologies. Do not automatically start another recording or move learners away from the completed audio before they can replay, review the transcript or revisit supporting materials.

[^active-learning]: [The testing effect and its role in learning](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1745-6924.2006.00012.x "Roediger, H. L., & Karpicke, J. D. (2006). The Power of Testing Memory: Basic Research and Implications for Educational Practice. Perspectives on Psychological Science, 1(3), 181–210. Retrieval after learning can strengthen long-term retention compared with additional study.")


{% /pattern-list %}

{% pattern-list role="checklist" layer="system" %}
# System design

I’d keep the title and make the requirement cover the **audio stream and all associated protected assets**, not merely the page containing the player. This parallels the existing video security criterion. 

## The audio is protected from unauthorised access

Restrict access according to the intended audience using authentication and role-based permissions. Verify authorisation whenever protected audio or related resources are requested, including streams, downloadable files, transcripts and localized versions. Do not rely on an unlisted page or difficult-to-guess file URL as an access-control mechanism.[^access-control]

Where audio files are delivered directly from object storage or a CDN, use appropriately restricted access such as short-lived signed URLs or equivalent mechanisms rather than permanently public asset URLs. Prevent unintended indexing and direct-link sharing when the recording is intended only for authenticated learners.

Keep access rules consistent across the learning page and the underlying media delivery system. When external hosting is used, verify that its privacy and sharing settings provide the level of protection required by the learning environment.

[^access-control]: [Authorization guidance](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html "OWASP Foundation. Authorization Cheat Sheet. Guidance includes denying access by default, validating permissions on every request and enforcing authorization at the appropriate application and resource layers.")

## Audio is reliably accessible in all target regions

Deliver audio through infrastructure that provides reliable access and adequate performance in every intended region. For geographically distributed audiences, use a CDN or equivalent delivery mechanism so media can be served close to learners and with reduced dependence on a distant origin server.[^cdn-delivery]

Test playback from representative target regions and network conditions. Verify that recordings load, start promptly, seek reliably and continue without excessive interruption, and confirm that authentication, signed URLs, CDN configuration or geographic restrictions do not unintentionally block authorised learners.[^geo-restrictions]

Where reliable streaming cannot be guaranteed, provide an appropriate fallback, such as an alternative delivery host or downloadable audio where licensing, security and the learning context permit it.

[^cdn-delivery]: [How CloudFront delivers content](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/HowCloudFrontWorks.html "Amazon Web Services. How CloudFront Delivers Content. CloudFront routes requests through globally distributed edge locations and regional caches to reduce latency and improve delivery performance.")

[^geo-restrictions]: [Restrict the geographic distribution of your content](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/georestrictions.html "Amazon Web Services. Restrict the Geographic Distribution of Your Content. CDN geographic restrictions can explicitly allow or deny access by country and therefore need to be checked against the intended audience.")

I’d keep the title and make the distinction explicit between **listening behaviour** and **learning evidence**, as in the equivalent video criterion. 

## Audio completion and listening analytics are available

Track a clearly defined completion event for each learner and provide aggregated analytics for the intended audience. Where useful, report starts, listening progress, completions, repeated sections, skips, drop-off points and playback errors.[^learning-analytics]

Treat completion and listening behaviour as engagement data—not evidence of attention, understanding or mastery. Use learning activities or assessments when evidence of comprehension or performance is required.

Collect identifiable listening analytics only for defined purposes, restrict access and retention, and use aggregated or pseudonymised data whenever individual records are unnecessary.[^data-protection]

[^learning-analytics]: [Caliper Analytics](https://www.1edtech.org/standards/caliper "1EdTech Consortium. Caliper Analytics: A Standard for Capturing and Exchanging Learning Activity and Product-Usage Data.")

[^data-protection]: [Principles of the GDPR](https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/information-business-and-organisations/principles-gdpr_en "European Commission. Principles of the General Data Protection Regulation, including purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation and security.")

For audio, I’d broaden the equivalent video item slightly: native mobile behaviour includes **interruptions, headphones/Bluetooth changes, lock screen/background playback and system media controls**, not just the in-page player.

## Native mobile playback is tested and preserves intended interactions

Test audio playback on supported iOS and Android devices, including the native browser or app playback environment. Verify play, pause, seeking, playback speed, chapters and other intended controls, and ensure that switching apps, locking the device or returning to the learning activity does not unexpectedly lose the playback position or surrounding activity state.[^android-media][^ios-interruptions]

Test common mobile interruptions and audio-route changes, including incoming calls, notifications, connecting or disconnecting headphones and switching Bluetooth devices. Playback should pause, continue or resume in a predictable way and must not unexpectedly switch private audio to the device speaker when headphones are disconnected.[^ios-routes][^android-media]

Where background playback is intentionally supported, ensure that learners can control it through appropriate system media controls and return to the learning activity without losing their position. Do not enable background playback when continued listening outside the activity would conflict with its intended learning interactions.

[^ios-interruptions]: [Handling audio interruptions](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/avfaudio/handling-audio-interruptions "Apple Developer Documentation. Guidance on responding to interruptions such as calls and system events and maintaining appropriate playback state.")

[^ios-routes]: [Responding to audio route changes](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/avfaudio/responding-to-audio-route-changes "Apple Developer Documentation. Guidance on handling changes between speakers, headphones and other audio routes, including pausing playback when headphones are disconnected.")

[^android-media]: [Media3 playback guidelines](https://developer.android.com/distribute/aep/aep-req-media-3 "Android Developers. Media3 playback guidance includes audio-focus management, hardware media controls, background playback, media resumption and graceful handling of headphone disconnection.")

{% /pattern-list %}

